From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 05:49:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: hand the Messaging app received files: file:// URIs,
 fetched by kind and size, readable by the app

* Give the app a received file as its file:// URI

A Telegram photo reached the Messaging app as the bare word "photo".
imlibpurpleservice gives libpurple no GdkPixbuf, so canShowImages() is
false and a downloaded photo went out as showGenericFileInline's link,
<a href="file://...">photo</a>. The transport keeps no link
(IMMessage::initFromCallback drops every tag but b, i, u and br), so
only the link's text was stored. A document, video or audio file
downloaded in a group, and a sticker, fared the same: a file name, or
the word "sticker".

Where the front-end is imlibpurpleservice, whose UI name is "Palm
Messaging", showGenericFileInline now gives the file's URI as plain
text, made by g_filename_to_uri as makeFileUri does, then the links to
other sizes, if any, and the caption on a line of its own. The app
shows a file:// URI as a picture, a player or a chip that opens the
file, as it does WhatsApp's. An animated sticker, which nothing there
draws, keeps the link it had; one with a thumbnail shows that.

A file that is still downloading when its message is shown, after the
wait for it, was shown in two messages: the caption, with a notice
"Downloading photo", and later the file. The transport keeps one
message for each id (IncomingIMHandler dedups on serviceMessageId), so
the second one, the file, was dropped wherever there was a caption.
Here the notice now goes alone, and the caption is kept on the
download's request and comes with the file. A message shown as the
account goes, whose download will not finish, keeps its caption with
the notice. A video's thumbnail is not given the app either: the
transport drops an imgstore picture, and the thumbnail would make the
notice a message of the same id.

Unit tests in test/webos-commands-test.cpp.

* Fetch a file by its kind and size, the rest on the app's tap

What was fetched was decided by size alone: the account's
media-size-threshold, 32 MB unless set. A larger file was to be asked
about with purple_request_action, which imlibpurpleservice does not
have (request_action is NULL in its request UI ops). The question was
never put or answered, so the file never came, the message, its
caption with it, was never shown, and the InlineDownloadInfo kept for
the answer was never freed. In a private chat the account's default
download behaviour, file-transfer where the UI is not Pidgin, offered a
document, video or music file as a PurpleXfer, which imlibpurpleservice
shows no one either.

Where the front-end is imlibpurpleservice, a file is now fetched where
it is shown, whatever the chat, by its kind and size
(webos::downloadsByItself): a photo, a sticker, a voice note and an
animation of any size, and a document of up to 1 MB
(webos::AutoDownloadDocumentMaxBytes, one constant). A video, a video
message, a music file, and a document over 1 MB or of no known size,
are not fetched. The message is shown at once with this as its first
line, and the caption, as markup, below it:

  luneos-file:?name=<file name, percent-encoded>&size=<bytes>&type=<video|audio|document>&state=ask

The Messaging app draws a card from it, and a tap sends the transport
downloadFile, which comes as the signal "webos-im-download-file"
(account, chat, serviceMessageId). The plug-in finds the message again
with getMessage, so a card from before a restart will do, rewrites the
line with state=downloading, then, at most every 3 s, with &got=<bytes>
as TDLib reports them (updateFile), and fetches the file. Once it is
here the message becomes the file's URI and the caption, as a file
fetched as it came is shown; if it does not come, state=failed, and a
later tap tries again. A forwarded message keeps the line saying so in
front, as it was shown. These rewrites go as "webos-im-replace"
(account, serviceMessageId, text), which the transport applies as it
does "webos-im-edit" but without marking the message edited: they are
not the sender's.

Another front-end without request_action is no longer asked about a
file above the limit, rather than asked by no one.

Unit tests in test/webos-commands-test.cpp. The 0040 test of a video
fetched after the wait now uses an animation, as a video waits for a
tap.

* Let the Messaging app read a received file

A received Telegram photo showed in the Messaging app as a broken
image. The app gets the file's file:// URI, but TDLib's files live in
/media/cryptofs/.purple-tdlib-files/<account>, which the plugin makes
0700 for root, and TDLib writes the files 0640 in 0750 folders. The app
runs in WebAppMgr as another user (wam), so it could open none of them;
a video only played because the media server, not the app, reads it. A
contact card's .vcf, kept in the same tree, could not be read either.

Where a file is handed over (makeFileUri, contactCardText), make it
readable for all and give each folder from its own up to the files root
search permission only (o+x), so that a file can be opened by its name
and no folder can be listed. The database (.purple-tdlib-db) is left
as it was. Only a regular file under the root, not reached through a
link or "..", is changed; it is opened with O_NOFOLLOW and changed
through its descriptor.

Unit test in test/webos-receive-test.cpp.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
